# Twitter Analytics and Monitoring

This guide shows how to generate a credential-free Twitter/X analytics report
from imported metric rows. The workflow is safe for CI and local agent review:
it does not call Twitter/X APIs, post content, send direct messages, or mutate an
account.

```elixir
alias Lux.Prisms.Twitter.GenerateAnalyticsReport

payload = %{
  account_id: "lux",
  tweets: [
    %{
      id: "launch",
      text: "Great launch thread for agents",
      impressions: 5_000,
      likes: 350,
      replies: 45,
      retweets: 80,
      quotes: 10,
      bookmarks: 40,
      hashtags: ["agents", "ai"]
    }
  ],
  mentions: [
    %{id: "m1", text: "thanks, very useful", followers_count: 700},
    %{id: "m2", text: "bad setup error", followers_count: 35_000}
  ],
  follower_snapshots: [
    %{timestamp: "2026-06-01", count: 2_500},
    %{timestamp: "2026-06-05", count: 2_680}
  ],
  alert_thresholds: %{negative_sentiment_above: 0.25}
}

{:ok, report} = GenerateAnalyticsReport.handler(payload, %{name: "AnalyticsAgent"})
report.report_sections
```

The report includes:

- aggregate impressions, engagements, engagement rate, and profile click rate
- per-tweet normalized metrics and data quality notes
- hashtag performance rollups
- follower growth deltas
- mention sentiment summaries with high-influence negative mention detection
- custom metric ratios
- deterministic alerts and dashboard-ready chart payloads

Live Twitter/X collection can be added later as a transport adapter that feeds
the same payload shape into this prism. Tests should continue to use fixture
payloads so credentials and account mutations are never required for review.
